    {
      "name": "GROWTH_STEP",
      "docs": [
        "The most an account may grow in one instruction. A code buffer past this is reached by repeating",
        "`grow_code_buffer`; the runtime measures the delta against the length at instruction entry, so one",
        "step per instruction is always legal."
      ],
      "type": "u32",
      "value": "10240"
    },
    {
      "name": "MAX_CODE_LEN",
      "docs": [
        "Largest program a code buffer may hold.",
        "",
        "Bounded by `finalize_code_buffer`, which hashes the whole program in **one instruction**:",
        "measured at 531,110 CU at this size, against the 1,400,000 a transaction can request — ~2.6x",
        "headroom, and `finalize_at_max_code_len_fits_the_compute_budget` asserts it. A cap above what",
        "finalize can hash would be worse than no cap: a program could be written across every chunk and",
        "then be impossible to finalize, the failure landing on the last instruction after all the cost",
        "was paid.",
        "",
        "Not the account ceiling. `MAX_PERMITTED_DATA_LENGTH` is 10 MiB, but finalize cannot hash that in",
        "one instruction, and the staging cost — one transaction per ~900-byte chunk, one",
        "`grow_code_buffer` per [`GROWTH_STEP`], and rent on a `len`-sized account for as long as the",
        "program lives — scales the whole way there.",
        "",
        "Nothing in the engine constrains it: bytecode is interpreted straight out of the account borrow",
        "and jump targets decode as `u32` against the slice length, so code length is unrelated to the",
        "engine's heap size. Raising it past the finalize ceiling means hashing incrementally across",
        "instructions and persisting the sha256 midstate here — a change to the staging protocol, not to",
        "this number."
      ],
      "type": "u32",
      "value": "1048576"
    },
    {
      "name": "MAX_POT_LEN",
      "docs": [
        "Largest Pot this program will mint. A Pot is created at its final size and can never grow —",
        "`realloc` belongs to the owning program, which is the engine — so this is a ceiling on a",
        "one-time choice rather than on a running total. Held at one `MAX_PERMITTED_DATA_INCREASE`",
        "because that is the most the System Program will allocate in a single instruction."
      ],
      "type": "u32",
      "value": "10240"
    },
    {
      "name": "POT_SEED",
      "type": "bytes",
      "value": "[112, 111, 116]"
    }
  ]
}